export const DATA_58: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-075", num: 75, title: "Đề 075", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Favorite Drinks and Healthy Habits", paragraphs: ["What we choose to drink every day has a bigger impact on our health than many people realise. While food gets most of the attention when it comes to healthy eating, the beverages we consume — from the first glass of water in the morning to the last cup of tea at night — play an equally important role in how we feel, how our bodies function, and how well we sleep. Developing good drinking habits is one of the simplest and most effective steps anyone can take to improve their overall well-being.", "Water is the most essential drink of all. The human body is made up of approximately sixty percent water, and we lose moisture constantly through breathing, sweating, and other bodily functions. Most health experts recommend drinking between six and eight glasses of water a day to stay properly hydrated. When a person does not drink enough water, they may experience headaches, difficulty concentrating, low energy, and dry skin. Despite how important water is, many people consistently drink less than they should, often because they prefer beverages with more flavour, such as soft drinks, fruit juices, or coffee. Health professionals often encourage people to carry a reusable water bottle with them throughout the day as a simple reminder to stay hydrated.", "Tea is one of the world's most popular drinks, consumed by billions of people in almost every country. Green tea, in particular, has attracted a great deal of scientific attention due to its high content of antioxidants — natural compounds that help protect the body's cells from damage. Studies suggest that regular consumption of green tea may reduce the risk of heart disease, support healthy brain function, and even help the body manage weight more effectively. Black tea and herbal teas also offer various health benefits: chamomile tea, for example, is widely used as a natural way to promote relaxation and improve sleep quality, while ginger tea is popular for its ability to ease digestive discomfort and reduce nausea.", "Coffee is another extremely popular beverage, and its relationship with health is more complex than many people assume. For years, coffee was considered unhealthy due to its high caffeine content, and people were often advised to drink it only in small amounts. However, more recent research has painted a more nuanced picture. Studies have found that moderate coffee consumption — roughly two to four cups per day — is associated with a reduced risk of certain diseases, including type 2 diabetes, Parkinson's disease, and liver conditions. Coffee also contains antioxidants and has been shown to improve alertness and concentration in the short term. The key, as with most things, is moderation: drinking too much coffee can lead to anxiety, sleep problems, and an elevated heart rate.", "Fruit juices and smoothies are often seen as healthy choices, but this perception can sometimes be misleading . While a freshly squeezed orange juice does contain vitamins and minerals, it also contains a significant amount of natural sugar and lacks the dietary fibre found in a whole piece of fruit. A large glass of fruit juice can contain as much sugar as a soft drink, which is why nutritionists often recommend eating the whole fruit instead of drinking its juice. Smoothies made at home with whole fruits, vegetables, and low-fat milk or yoghurt are generally a healthier option, as they retain the fibre content and provide a broader range of nutrients.", "Milk is another drink with a strong health reputation, particularly for its role in building and maintaining healthy bones. Milk and dairy products are rich sources of calcium, vitamin D, and protein, all of which are important for bone strength, muscle function, and overall growth. This is why nutritionists particularly recommend milk for children and teenagers, whose bones are still developing. Plant-based alternatives such as oat milk, almond milk, and soy milk have grown in popularity in recent years, especially among people who are lactose intolerant or who follow a vegan diet. Many of these alternatives are now fortified with calcium and vitamin D to bring their nutritional profile closer to that of dairy milk.", "Developing healthy drinking habits does not require dramatic changes. Simple steps — such as starting each day with a glass of water, choosing herbal tea over a second cup of coffee in the evening, and opting for a smoothie instead of a soft drink — can make a real difference to how you feel over time. The most important principle is to pay attention to what you are drinking and to choose beverages that nourish rather than harm the body. Small, consistent choices about what we drink add up to meaningful long-term benefits for our health and energy levels."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["A discussion of how different everyday drinks affect health and how to develop better drinking habits", "A scientific study of the long-term effects of coffee and tea on the human brain", "A comparison of the most popular beverages sold in supermarkets around the world", "An explanation of why water is the only truly healthy drink for the human body"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what can happen when a person does not drink enough water?", options: ["Their digestive system will slow down, causing weight gain and bloating", "They may experience headaches, difficulty concentrating, low energy, and dry skin", "They may develop serious kidney or liver problems that require medical treatment", "They will immediately feel thirsty and naturally drink more to compensate"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is chamomile tea most commonly used for?", options: ["Promoting relaxation and helping people achieve better quality sleep", "Easing digestive discomfort and reducing feelings of nausea after meals", "Reducing the risk of heart disease and supporting healthy brain function", "Providing a source of antioxidants that protect the body's cells from damage"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does recent research suggest about moderate coffee consumption?", options: ["It is associated with a reduced risk of certain diseases including type 2 diabetes and Parkinson's disease", "It causes long-term damage to the liver and significantly increases the risk of heart disease", "It should be completely avoided by people over the age of forty to prevent anxiety and sleep problems", "It has no measurable effect on health and its popularity is entirely based on taste preference"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern about drinking fruit juice?", options: ["Fruit juice can trigger allergic reactions in people sensitive to certain fruits", "Fruit juice lacks the dietary fibre found in a whole piece of fruit", "Fruit juice contains a significant amount of natural sugar", "A large glass of fruit juice may contain as much sugar as a soft drink"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are plant-based milk alternatives often fortified with calcium and vitamin D?", options: ["To allow them to be sold at a lower price than dairy milk while still meeting government nutrition standards", "To make them taste more similar to dairy milk so that children are more willing to drink them", "To replace the protein lost during the manufacturing process of producing plant-based milk products", "To bring their nutritional profile closer to that of dairy milk for people who cannot or do not consume it"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["reusable water bottles recommended as a convenient way to track daily fluid intake", "beverages with more flavour that many people prefer over plain water", "health professionals who advise patients on staying hydrated throughout the day", "people who are encouraged to carry a water bottle as a reminder to drink more"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["chamomile tea, which promotes relaxation and improves sleep quality", "black tea, which is described as having various health benefits alongside herbal teas", "green tea, which has attracted scientific attention due to its high antioxidant content", "ginger tea, which is popular for its ability to ease digestive discomfort and reduce nausea"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"misleading\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["strongly disagreed with by the majority of nutrition scientists and health professionals", "giving a false or inaccurate impression that does not reflect the true situation", "outdated and no longer supported by current scientific research", "unnecessarily complicated and difficult to understand without expert guidance"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward improving drinking habits?", options: ["Discouraging — the author implies that most people will never successfully change their drinking habits", "Encouraging and practical — the author believes small, consistent choices can make a meaningful difference", "Indifferent — the author presents information about drinks without suggesting any personal recommendations", "Strict — the author insists that only water and herbal tea should be consumed for good health"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Lifestyle of Generation Z", paragraphs: ["Generation Z — broadly defined as those born between the mid-1990s and the early 2010s — is the first generation to have grown up entirely in the digital age. Unlike their predecessors, the Millennials, who experienced the transition from analogue to digital as teenagers or young adults, members of Generation Z have never known a world without smartphones, social media, or instant access to information. This fundamental difference in upbringing has shaped their values, habits, social behaviours, and expectations of the world around them in ways that researchers, employers, and marketers are still working to fully understand.", "Technology is, unsurprisingly, central to almost every aspect of Generation Z's daily life. Studies show that the average member of this generation spends several hours each day on a smartphone — using it for communication, entertainment, shopping, learning, and social interaction. Social media platforms such as Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, and Snapchat serve not just as entertainment channels but as primary sources of news, product discovery, and cultural reference. Unlike older generations who tend to value a clear separation between online and offline life, many members of Generation Z experience these two worlds as seamlessly integrated, with digital relationships and experiences carrying the same emotional weight as face-to-face interactions.", "Mental health is a defining concern for Generation Z. Research consistently shows that this generation reports higher rates of anxiety, depression, and loneliness than previous generations at the same age — a finding that has prompted considerable debate about its causes. Some researchers point directly to the effects of heavy social media use, arguing that constant comparison with idealised online images, exposure to cyberbullying, and the pressure to maintain a curated public persona contribute significantly to psychological distress. Others caution against over-simplified explanations, pointing out that Generation Z is also more aware of mental health issues, more likely to seek help, and more willing to talk openly about psychological struggles than any previous generation — which may partly explain the higher reported rates.", "Generation Z's relationship with work and career expectations differs markedly from that of earlier generations. Raised during the global financial crisis of 2008 and coming of age during the COVID-19 pandemic, many members of this generation have developed a pragmatic rather than idealistic attitude toward employment. They tend to value job security, work-life balance, and mental health support from employers, and are generally more likely than Millennials to prioritise financial stability over passion-driven career choices. At the same time, they are highly attuned to questions of corporate ethics and social responsibility: research shows that a significant majority of Generation Z consumers and workers prefer to support companies whose values align with their own on issues such as sustainability, diversity, and social justice.", "Consumption habits within Generation Z show some distinctive patterns. Contrary to the stereotype that younger generations are irresponsible with money, surveys suggest that many members of Generation Z are more financially cautious than Millennials were at the same age — a tendency often attributed to the economic insecurity they witnessed or experienced during their formative years. Second-hand shopping, subscription services, and experience-based spending tend to be favoured over the ownership of large numbers of material possessions. Sustainability is an important factor in the purchasing decisions of many Generation Z consumers, with studies showing that a substantial proportion of this group actively avoids brands perceived as environmentally harmful or socially irresponsible.", "Social and political engagement among Generation Z is often characterised by a preference for direct, grassroots action over participation in formal political institutions. Movements such as the global climate strikes of 2019, inspired largely by Swedish activist Greta Thunberg, drew enormous participation from young people around the world and demonstrated their capacity for large-scale coordinated action on issues they care deeply about. However, research also suggests that many members of Generation Z feel a degree of disillusionment with traditional political parties and established institutions, which they often perceive as slow-moving, disconnected from everyday realities, and insufficiently responsive to the urgency of challenges such as climate change and social inequality.", "Understanding Generation Z is important not only for those seeking to market products or design workplaces, but for anyone trying to anticipate how societies will function as this generation assumes greater economic, cultural, and political influence. Their experiences have shaped a cohort that is simultaneously more connected and more anxious, more pragmatic and more idealistic, more diverse and more globally aware than any generation before them. How they navigate the challenges of adulthood — and what they demand of the institutions they inherit — will shape the world for decades to come."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To compare the mental health of Generation Z with that of Millennials using scientific research data", "To provide a broad overview of the values, habits, and challenges that define the lifestyle of Generation Z", "To argue that Generation Z is more technologically skilled than any previous generation in history", "To explain why employers should change their workplace policies to attract Generation Z workers"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do many Generation Z members experience the relationship between online and offline life?", options: ["They prefer to keep their online and offline lives completely separate to protect their privacy", "They use social media primarily for entertainment and do not consider it a meaningful source of news or cultural reference", "They find online relationships less satisfying than face-to-face interactions and use social media reluctantly", "They experience the two worlds as seamlessly integrated, with digital interactions carrying the same emotional weight as physical ones"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor contributing to mental health challenges among Generation Z?", options: ["Exposure to cyberbullying through digital platforms", "Constant comparison with idealised images on social media platforms", "Pressure to maintain a carefully managed public persona online", "Reduced access to professional mental health services in schools and universities"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has shaped Generation Z's pragmatic attitude toward employment?", options: ["Witnessing their parents lose passion-driven careers due to a lack of workplace flexibility", "Growing up during the global financial crisis and coming of age during the COVID-19 pandemic", "Being influenced by Millennial colleagues who encouraged them to prioritise financial security", "Receiving poor career guidance from schools and universities during their formative years"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes Generation Z's approach to consumption?", options: ["They rely entirely on subscription services and have largely abandoned the practice of owning any physical products", "Many are financially cautious, favour second-hand shopping and experiences, and consider sustainability in their purchases", "They tend to spend freely on luxury goods and experiences, showing little concern for financial security", "They prefer to own large numbers of possessions and rarely purchase second-hand items"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does Generation Z's participation in the 2019 climate strikes demonstrate?", options: ["Their capacity to organise and participate in large-scale coordinated action on issues they care about", "Their rejection of all forms of environmental regulation in favour of individual lifestyle choices", "That young people are more willing to vote in national elections than any previous generation", "That Greta Thunberg was solely responsible for inspiring a worldwide movement among young people"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["grassroots movements that prefer direct action over engagement with political parties", "traditional political parties that have been insufficiently responsive to climate change", "young people around the world who participated in the 2019 climate strikes", "formal political institutions that Generation Z finds slow-moving and disconnected"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["social media platforms such as Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, and Snapchat", "the online and offline worlds that many Generation Z members experience as integrated", "digital relationships that Generation Z considers less meaningful than face-to-face interactions", "entertainment channels that older generations use to separate their personal and professional lives"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"attuned\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["formally trained in and professionally qualified for", "confused by and unwilling to engage with", "opposed to and actively resistant toward", "sensitively aware of and responsive to"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about Generation Z's relationship with traditional political institutions?", options: ["Generation Z has no interest in political issues and focuses entirely on personal financial security and career development", "Generation Z fully supports traditional political parties and views them as the most effective vehicle for social change", "Many members of Generation Z feel let down by established institutions and prefer to pursue change through direct grassroots action", "The majority of Generation Z refuses to vote in elections because they believe democratic systems are fundamentally corrupt"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Evolution and Integration of AI Tools", paragraphs: ["Artificial intelligence, once the exclusive domain of research laboratories and technology companies with substantial computational resources, has undergone a transformation in the past decade so rapid and far-reaching that its effects are now visible across virtually every sector of modern economic and social life. The progression from narrow, task-specific programs — capable of performing one function well but nothing beyond it — to large-scale systems capable of generating text, images, audio, and code across a vast range of domains represents a qualitative shift in what machine intelligence can do and, correspondingly, in how societies must think about its role. Understanding this evolution requires attention not only to the technologies themselves but to the institutional, economic, and ethical contexts in which they have developed and through which their consequences are being negotiated.", "The foundational shift in AI development came with the rise of deep learning — a family of machine learning techniques inspired by the structure of biological neural networks. Early AI systems relied on explicit, hand-crafted rules: programmers specified what the system should do in response to every anticipated input. Deep learning systems, by contrast, derive their capabilities from exposure to enormous quantities of training data, adjusting millions or billions of internal parameters through iterative optimisation until the system can perform a given task with high accuracy. This approach proved transformative for tasks involving pattern recognition — particularly speech, image, and text — and produced dramatic improvements in fields from medical diagnostics to autonomous vehicle navigation. The availability of large datasets, increases in computational power, and advances in optimisation algorithms have collectively driven a compounding cycle of improvement that shows no clear sign of levelling off.", "The integration of AI tools into professional and institutional settings has followed an uneven trajectory. In some domains — financial services, logistics, and pharmaceutical research, for example — AI adoption has been rapid and has produced measurable gains in efficiency, accuracy, and the ability to process information at scales previously impossible for human analysts. In others, particularly those involving complex human judgment, interpersonal relationships, or high-stakes decisions with ethical dimensions — such as criminal justice, healthcare diagnosis, and social work — the pace of integration has been more cautious and more contested. [1] Critics in these domains argue that opaque algorithmic systems imported into high-stakes decision environments may encode and amplify existing biases, reduce accountability, and displace the exercise of professional judgment in ways that are difficult to detect and harder to reverse. [2] Proponents counter that human decision-making in these same environments is also subject to bias, inconsistency, and cognitive limitation, and that well-designed AI tools can reduce rather than exacerbate these problems. [3] Adjudicating between these positions requires empirical evidence that, in many cases, is still being gathered. [4]", "The economic implications of AI integration are among the most debated dimensions of the technology's societal impact. Labour market analysts have long warned that automation could displace significant numbers of workers in routine-cognitive and manual roles, and early evidence from sectors where AI tools have been most extensively deployed suggests that these concerns are not unfounded. At the same time, historical precedent — from the mechanisation of agriculture to the computerisation of clerical work — suggests that technological displacement of this kind tends, over time, to generate new categories of employment even as it eliminates others. Whether the pace of AI-driven displacement will allow sufficient time for workers and educational institutions to adapt, and whether the new employment generated will be accessible to those most affected by displacement, are questions that remain genuinely open. What seems clear is that the distribution of AI's economic benefits and costs will not be neutral: those with advanced technical skills, access to quality education, and positions in sectors that complement rather than compete with AI capabilities are likely to benefit disproportionately.", "The governance of AI has emerged as a significant challenge for policymakers worldwide. The development of powerful AI systems is concentrated in a small number of large technology companies and state research programmes, primarily in the United States and China, creating an asymmetry of capability and influence that raises concerns about market concentration, national security, and the governance of technologies whose effects are inherently global. The European Union has been among the most proactive legislative actors, enacting the AI Act — the world's first comprehensive legal framework for AI — which classifies AI applications by risk level and imposes corresponding requirements for transparency, accuracy, and human oversight. Whether this regulatory approach will prove effective, or whether the pace of AI development will consistently outrun the capacity of legislative processes to respond, remains to be seen.", "Questions of fairness, transparency, and accountability in AI systems have moved from academic discussion to mainstream policy concern with remarkable speed. Algorithmic systems used in contexts ranging from credit scoring and hiring to medical diagnosis and predictive policing have been shown to produce outcomes that differ systematically across demographic groups — outcomes that may reflect biases in training data, in the choice of what to optimise for, or in the deployment context itself. The field of \"responsible AI\" or \"AI ethics\" has grown rapidly in response, producing a substantial body of technical work on bias detection, model interpretability, and fairness metrics, alongside philosophical and legal scholarship on accountability and the appropriate limits of automated decision-making.", "The trajectory of AI development over the coming decades remains deeply uncertain. Scenarios range from the gradual and manageable integration of increasingly capable tools into existing institutions, to more disruptive possibilities involving systems whose capabilities significantly exceed those of human experts across a wide range of domains. Navigating this uncertainty responsibly requires sustained investment in research, adaptive regulatory frameworks, and — perhaps most importantly — deliberate attention to the question of whose interests AI development is ultimately designed to serve. Technology rarely determines its own social consequences; those are shaped by the choices, values, and power dynamics of the institutions and societies that develop and deploy it."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central focus of the passage?", options: ["An argument that AI development should be halted until comprehensive global regulations are established", "A technical guide to how deep learning algorithms are designed and trained", "A comparison of AI development programmes in the United States, China, and the European Union", "A broad analytical account of AI's evolution, its integration into institutions, and the governance challenges it raises"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do deep learning systems differ fundamentally from early AI systems?", options: ["Deep learning systems use hand-crafted rules written by programmers, while early AI systems learned from data", "Deep learning systems derive their capabilities from training data rather than from explicit rules programmed by humans", "Deep learning systems require less computational power and can operate on smaller datasets than earlier approaches", "Deep learning systems are limited to tasks involving images and audio, unlike earlier AI which could handle text"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern about integrating AI into high-stakes decision environments?", options: ["AI integration may displace professional judgment in ways that are difficult to detect or reverse", "Algorithmic systems may encode and amplify existing biases present in training data", "The deployment of AI may lead to higher operational costs that smaller institutions cannot afford", "AI tools may reduce accountability by making the basis for decisions harder to scrutinise"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to paragraphs 2 and 4 taken together, what pattern does the author identify regarding technology and employment?", options: ["The mechanisation and computerisation of previous eras suggest that AI may ultimately generate new job categories even as it eliminates others", "Historical precedent shows that technological change has never displaced workers in cognitive roles, only in manual ones", "Technological advances have historically caused permanent and irreversible unemployment in every sector they affect", "AI will create more jobs than it eliminates because it requires vast numbers of human operators to function correctly"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the significance of the European Union's AI Act?", options: ["It is the first comprehensive legal framework globally to classify AI applications by risk and impose corresponding requirements", "It requires all AI systems used in Europe to be designed and built by companies registered in EU member states", "It prohibits the use of AI in any application that could affect individual rights or freedoms", "It establishes a joint regulatory body with the United States and China to coordinate global AI governance"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have studies found about algorithmic systems used in contexts such as credit scoring and predictive policing?", options: ["They have produced systematically different outcomes across demographic groups, raising concerns about embedded bias", "They have been shown to completely eliminate human bias in high-stakes institutional decisions", "Their effectiveness has been widely praised by both technical researchers and policymakers as a model for responsible AI deployment", "These systems have proven to be more accurate and less biased than human decision-makers in all tested contexts"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["technology companies and research laboratories with substantial computational resources", "the institutional, economic, and ethical contexts in which AI has developed", "societies that must think carefully about the role of machine intelligence", "large-scale AI systems capable of generating text, images, audio, and code"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["historical precedents such as agricultural mechanisation and the computerisation of clerical work", "people with advanced technical skills and access to quality education in AI-complementary sectors", "questions about whether AI-driven displacement will allow workers and institutions time to adapt", "labour market analysts who have warned about the risks of automation-driven unemployment"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward AI integration in society?", options: ["Politically partisan — the author supports EU regulatory approaches and is critical of the US and Chinese models", "Uncritically enthusiastic — the author presents AI as an unambiguous force for progress that should be adopted as quickly as possible", "Deeply pessimistic — the author implies that AI will inevitably cause more harm than benefit to most people", "Analytically measured — the author acknowledges AI's significant potential while emphasising that its social consequences depend on human choices and governance"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 3? 'The concerns centre not on efficiency but on the adequacy of existing accountability structures when consequential decisions are delegated, in whole or in part, to automated systems.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Frontier of Energy Regeneration and Sustainable Systems", paragraphs: ["The global transition away from fossil fuels toward sustainable energy systems is frequently framed in public discourse as a straightforward technological substitution — a matter of replacing coal, oil, and gas with solar panels, wind turbines, and batteries. This framing, while politically useful for mobilising broad coalitions of support, obscures the genuine complexity of the transformation underway: a transformation that involves not merely the replacement of one energy source with another, but the reconstruction of the physical, institutional, and economic infrastructure through which modern societies produce, distribute, and consume energy. Among the most technically and conceptually challenging dimensions of this reconstruction is the problem of energy storage and regeneration — the challenge of capturing energy when it is produced in excess and returning it to the grid in a form and at a time when it is needed. How this challenge is resolved will shape not only the pace but the character of the energy transition itself.", "The intermittency of renewable energy sources is the foundational constraint from which most energy storage challenges derive. Solar photovoltaic systems generate electricity only when sunlight is available; wind turbines produce power only when wind speeds fall within an operational range. Unlike fossil fuel plants, which can be dispatched on demand to meet whatever load the grid requires, renewable generators are subject to natural variability that is partly predictable but never fully controllable. In regions where renewable penetration is high, the mismatch between supply and demand can produce periods of significant surplus — when generation exceeds consumption and prices on wholesale electricity markets may fall to zero or even turn negative — alongside periods of shortage when renewable output drops and backup capacity must be called upon. Managing this variability without relying on fossil fuel backup is the central technical challenge of the high-renewable grid.", "Lithium-ion battery technology, the dominant form of electrochemical storage currently deployed at both grid and consumer scale, has undergone dramatic cost reductions over the past fifteen years — a decline of more than 90 percent in inflation-adjusted terms since 2010 — driven by the parallel growth of the electric vehicle market and sustained investment in manufacturing capacity. These cost reductions have made battery storage economically viable for a range of applications that were previously impractical, including short-duration grid balancing, behind-the-meter storage for commercial and residential consumers, and the replacement of fast-ramping gas peaker plants in some electricity markets. However, lithium-ion technology faces inherent limitations in the duration of storage it can provide economically: at current costs, deploying sufficient battery capacity to store several days of grid-scale energy — the level required to manage prolonged periods of low wind and solar output — remains prohibitively expensive and faces constraints from the availability of lithium, cobalt, and other critical minerals.", "Long-duration energy storage technologies are therefore attracting growing research and commercial investment as a complement or successor to short-duration battery systems. Among the most promising candidates are flow batteries, which store energy in liquid electrolytes that can be held in large tanks and scaled independently of the power conversion system; hydrogen electrolysis and fuel cell systems, which use surplus electricity to split water into hydrogen and oxygen and later recombine them to generate power; and compressed air energy storage, which uses excess electricity to compress air into underground caverns or tanks and releases it through turbines when power is needed. Each of these approaches faces its own set of technical and economic challenges — low round-trip efficiency, high capital cost, or dependence on specific geological conditions — and none has yet demonstrated the combination of cost, scalability, and reliability required for widespread deployment.", "Demand-side flexibility represents an underexplored but potentially powerful complement to supply-side storage. Rather than building ever-larger storage systems to accommodate fixed patterns of electricity demand, demand-side flexibility involves shifting the timing of consumption — the charging of electric vehicles, the operation of industrial processes, the heating and cooling of buildings — to align more closely with periods of high renewable generation. Smart grid technologies, real-time pricing signals, and the aggregation of distributed flexible loads through virtual power plant architectures are enabling a degree of demand responsiveness that was not achievable with earlier grid infrastructure. If demand-side flexibility can be deployed at sufficient scale, it may reduce the quantity of storage required to maintain grid stability, lowering the overall cost of the energy transition.", "The governance of energy systems adds a further layer of complexity. Electricity markets in most countries were designed around centralised, despatchable generation, and their regulatory structures — including grid codes, capacity markets, and pricing mechanisms — do not always accommodate the characteristics of distributed, variable renewable generation or the diverse range of storage technologies now entering the market. Reforming these structures to facilitate the integration of new technologies while maintaining grid reliability and protecting consumer interests is a task that requires sustained regulatory attention and, in many cases, politically difficult decisions about who bears the cost of transition. International coordination on energy system standards, cross-border grid interconnection, and the governance of critical mineral supply chains adds further dimensions to a governance challenge of considerable scope and urgency.", "The energy systems of the mid-twenty-first century will likely look profoundly different from those of today — not merely in the fuels they use but in their architecture, their control systems, and the balance of power between centralised utilities and distributed prosumers: individuals and organisations that both produce and consume energy. Whether this transformation unfolds rapidly or slowly, equitably or unequally, in ways that strengthen or undermine democratic governance, will depend less on which technologies prove most effective than on the regulatory, financial, and political choices made in the years immediately ahead."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The energy transition involves profound technical, institutional, and governance challenges that extend far beyond simply switching to renewable energy sources", "International coordination is the only missing element needed to complete the transition to a fully sustainable global energy system", "Lithium-ion batteries are insufficient for the energy transition and should be replaced immediately by hydrogen fuel cell systems", "Renewable energy sources such as solar and wind are now fully capable of replacing fossil fuels without any additional infrastructure changes"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the cost reductions in lithium-ion battery technology that occurred since 2010", "lithium-ion batteries that have been deployed at both grid and consumer scale worldwide", "electric vehicles whose market growth has driven investment in battery manufacturing", "the applications such as grid balancing and behind-the-meter storage that batteries now enable"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what two factors are primarily credited with driving the dramatic reduction in lithium-ion battery costs since 2010?", options: ["Government subsidies for battery research and mandatory renewable energy targets in major economies", "The parallel growth of the electric vehicle market and sustained investment in manufacturing capacity", "Competition between Chinese and American battery manufacturers and the reduction of import tariffs on raw materials", "Advances in lithium extraction techniques and the discovery of large new cobalt deposits in Africa"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraphs 3 and 4 together about why long-duration storage technologies are receiving increased investment?", options: ["Lithium-ion batteries have been shown to be dangerous and are being phased out for safety reasons", "Lithium-ion technology cannot economically provide the multi-day storage capacity needed to manage prolonged low-renewable periods, creating a gap that other technologies must fill", "Regulators in most countries have mandated that grid operators stop using lithium-ion storage within five years", "Long-duration technologies have already proven cheaper than lithium-ion batteries for all grid-scale applications and are replacing them rapidly"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does demand-side flexibility differ in approach from building larger storage systems?", options: ["It replaces smart grid infrastructure with simpler fixed-rate pricing to encourage consumers to reduce peak-time usage", "Rather than storing surplus energy, it shifts the timing of consumption to align with periods of high renewable output", "It relies entirely on electric vehicle batteries as distributed storage rather than any centralised grid infrastructure", "It reduces the total amount of electricity consumed by households and businesses in order to lower grid stress"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does integrating new energy technologies into existing electricity markets present a governance challenge?", options: ["Because international energy governance bodies have imposed uniform rules that prevent individual countries from adapting their markets", "Because electricity consumers have consistently opposed the market reforms needed to support renewable integration", "Because renewable energy companies refuse to participate in regulated markets and prefer to operate outside existing grid structures", "Because existing regulatory structures were designed for centralised despatchable generation and do not readily accommodate variable distributed resources"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a long-duration energy storage technology under development?", options: ["Flow batteries that store energy in liquid electrolytes held in large tanks", "Hydrogen electrolysis systems that use surplus electricity to produce and later reconvert hydrogen", "Compressed air storage that uses excess electricity to compress air into underground spaces", "Thermal energy storage systems that capture heat in molten salt or other high-capacity materials"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the smart grid technology enabling real-time pricing signals across the electricity network", "the virtual power plant architecture that aggregates distributed flexible energy loads", "demand-side flexibility, which may reduce the quantity of storage needed to maintain grid stability", "the electric vehicle charging infrastructure that supports flexible load management in urban areas"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the author's overall perspective on the energy transition?", options: ["Nostalgic — the author implies that returning to centralised fossil fuel systems would be more reliable than pursuing distributed renewable generation", "Analytically rigorous — the author presents the energy transition as a complex, multi-dimensional challenge whose outcome depends on technical, regulatory, and political choices", "Optimistic — the author believes the technical solutions already exist and the transition will be completed rapidly", "Dismissive — the author treats renewable energy targets as politically motivated and scientifically unrealistic"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"promising\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["showing signs of potential future success but not yet fully demonstrated at scale", "already proven to be commercially viable and ready for widespread deployment", "regarded by all experts as superior to existing lithium-ion battery alternatives", "legally guaranteed to receive government funding and regulatory support"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-142", num: 142, title: "Đề 142", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Raising Children to Speak Two Languages", paragraphs: ["More and more parents around the world are now choosing to raise their children speaking two languages from birth. In the past, some experts worried that hearing two languages at home might confuse a young child and slow down learning. Today, however, most researchers believe that growing up bilingual brings several advantages, including stronger thinking skills, better communication, and more job opportunities later in life. Surveys in several countries show that a majority of parents now consider bilingualism a valuable skill rather than a risk. Because of this shift in opinion, bilingual parenting has become far more common in many countries over the last two decades.", "One popular method that many bilingual families use is called \"one parent, one language,\" often shortened to OPOL. Under this method, each parent speaks only a single language to the child, no matter what the topic or situation is. For instance, a mother might always speak Spanish to her son, while the father always speaks English, even when the whole family is together at the dinner table. Supporters of OPOL argue that this clear separation helps a child link one language firmly with one person, which can make early learning feel less confusing than mixing both languages randomly.", "Raising a bilingual child, however, is rarely completely smooth. Many young children mix words from both languages within a single sentence, a habit that can worry parents who fear their child is falling behind. Some bilingual toddlers also begin speaking full sentences a few months later than children raised with only one language. Despite these concerns, most language specialists agree that such patterns are normal and temporary; by the time children start primary school, bilingual and monolingual children typically reach similar levels of language ability. Doctors and speech therapists often reassure worried parents that this brief delay is not a sign of any real learning problem.", "Beyond simple communication, learning two languages early appears to offer children additional mental advantages. Several studies suggest that bilingual children become quicker at switching between different tasks, a skill researchers call cognitive flexibility. Being fluent in two languages may also give children a broader appreciation of other cultures, since language and culture are closely tied together in daily life. In addition, many bilingual adults report feeling more confident when traveling or working abroad. Some parents also point to future career advantages, noting that companies increasingly value employees who can speak more than one language; today, businesses often need staff who communicate easily with international clients, and recruiters say they actively search for such candidates.", "Keeping a child's weaker language strong usually requires steady exposure outside the home as well. Reading picture books, watching cartoons, and chatting regularly with grandparents or relatives in that language all give children extra practice. In recent years, a growing number of schools have introduced dual-language programs, where roughly half of each school day is taught in one language and half in the other, giving students structured opportunities to read and write in both. Community events, weekend language classes, and even video calls with distant family members can further add to this exposure.", "Language experts generally agree that starting early and staying consistent matters more than strictly following any single method. Families who weave both languages into everyday conversation, storytelling, and simple daily routines tend to raise children who grow up feeling comfortable and confident switching between the two languages whenever they need to."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Raising children bilingually offers several benefits despite some early challenges.", "Bilingual children face more learning problems than they gain advantages from speaking two languages.", "The one-parent-one-language method is the only effective way to raise a bilingual child.", "All schools now require students to learn a second language starting in kindergarten."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the name of the method in which each parent speaks only one language to the child?", options: ["One Parent, One Language (OPOL)", "Dual-Language Method", "Two-Language Rule", "Total Immersion"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What do many young bilingual children often do when speaking?", options: ["Mix words from both languages in one sentence", "Repeat words several times", "Speak only in their weaker language", "Refuse to speak either language"], answer: 0 }, { q: "In dual-language school programs, how is the school day usually divided?", options: ["Only special classes are taught in the second language", "One month is spent in each language", "Roughly half of each school day is taught in one language and half in the other", "Mornings are taught in one language and weekends in another"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of raising a bilingual child?", options: ["Improved memory for numbers and math", "Broader understanding of other cultures", "More career opportunities in the future", "Better cognitive flexibility"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"consistent\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["sudden", "strict", "steady", "separate"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"exposure\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["method", "protection", "contact", "practice"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["recruiters", "companies", "employees", "international clients"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what can be inferred about children in dual-language school programs?", options: ["They mainly attend these programs because their parents did not use the one-parent-one-language method at home.", "They can likely continue developing both languages by building on skills learned as young bilingual speakers, supported by structured school practice.", "They typically outperform monolingual children in every school subject by the time they reach secondary school.", "They will likely need less overall practice than children raised with only one language, since school alone provides enough exposure."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward raising children bilingually, as expressed in the passage?", options: ["Cautiously positive, acknowledging early challenges but emphasizing long-term benefits", "Purely descriptive of one school's dual-language program only", "Strongly critical, warning parents against attempting it", "Neutral but skeptical about whether the cognitive benefits are real"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Traditional Healing Practices and Global Recognition", paragraphs: ["In recent decades, a number of traditional health practices have gained formal recognition on the international stage, most notably through inscription on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. This growing recognition reflects a wider shift in global attitudes, as many societies have come to view these practices not merely as folk remedies but as valuable cultural traditions worth safeguarding for future generations. Rather than being viewed as outdated superstition, these traditions are increasingly appreciated for offering insights that complement, rather than compete with, modern medical science.", "Yoga, an ancient discipline that originated in India, was added to the UNESCO list in 2016. The practice combines physical postures, controlled breathing, and meditation techniques, all aimed at promoting balance between the body and mind. Although yoga has become closely associated with fitness studios across the Western world, its roots lie in a much broader philosophical tradition concerned with achieving inner calm and spiritual awareness, rather than simply improving flexibility.", "Acupuncture, a central technique within traditional Chinese medicine, received UNESCO recognition in 2010. Practitioners insert fine needles into specific points on the body, believed to correspond to pathways known as meridians, in order to restore the flow of vital energy, or qi. While some modern medical researchers remain cautious about the underlying theory, numerous clinical studies have examined acupuncture's potential effectiveness in managing chronic pain, and several health systems now offer it as a complementary treatment alongside conventional medicine. Some hospitals in Europe and North America have begun integrating acupuncture into pain-management clinics, although insurance coverage still varies considerably from country to country.", "Traditional Thai massage, locally known as Nuad Thai, was the most recent of the three to be inscribed , joining the UNESCO list in 2019. Unlike many Western massage styles that primarily rely on kneading soft tissue, Nuad Thai combines rhythmic pressure along energy lines with assisted stretching, drawing the recipient's body into positions reminiscent of yoga poses. Practitioners typically use their hands, elbows, knees, and feet, and sessions are traditionally performed without oil, with the recipient remaining fully clothed.", "Comparative studies of traditional healing systems often highlight this convergence, even when the practices developed independently in different regions of Asia. Despite their distinct techniques, these three traditions share a common underlying philosophy: they each treat physical health, mental well-being, and energy flow as deeply interconnected, rather than isolating symptoms from the person experiencing them. Yoga achieves this largely through self-directed movement and breath control, acupuncture through targeted needle insertion performed by a trained practitioner, and Thai massage through hands-on manipulation of the body by another person. This variation illustrates how differing cultural contexts have shaped distinct methods of pursuing a strikingly similar goal, even though they emerged in separate regions of Asia.", "UNESCO recognition carries practical consequences beyond mere prestige. Inscription can encourage governments to fund training programs, document traditional knowledge that might otherwise be lost as older practitioners retire, and protect authentic practices from being diluted by unregulated commercial imitations. Some scholars argue that recognition alone cannot guarantee genuine preservation unless local communities remain actively involved in teaching younger generations. Critics, however, caution that international popularity sometimes leads to oversimplified or commercialized versions of these traditions, stripped of the cultural and spiritual context in which they originally developed.", "Looking ahead, demand for these traditional therapies shows little sign of slowing, as increasing numbers of people worldwide seek alternatives or complements to conventional medical treatment. Whether this growing popularity will help preserve the practices' original depth, or gradually reshape them into something more commercialized, remains a question that scholars and practitioners continue to debate."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage describes how three traditional Asian healing practices gained UNESCO recognition and shows how they share an underlying philosophy despite using different techniques.", "The passage explains how yoga alone became internationally popular after receiving UNESCO recognition in 2016.", "The passage shows that yoga, acupuncture, and Thai massage were all developed in the same century by related communities.", "The passage argues that commercial exploitation is the greatest danger currently facing traditional Asian healing practices."], answer: 0 }, { q: "In which year was acupuncture added to the UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage list?", options: ["2016", "2005", "2019", "2010"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is a key difference between Nuad Thai and many Western massage styles?", options: ["Nuad Thai is typically performed using large amounts of oil, unlike most Western massage styles.", "Nuad Thai was developed specifically as a form of exercise for athletes preparing for competition.", "Nuad Thai combines pressure along energy lines with assisted stretching, while many Western styles mainly knead soft tissue.", "Nuad Thai relies primarily on kneading soft tissue, while Western styles use assisted stretching techniques."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what is one practical benefit that UNESCO recognition can bring to a traditional practice?", options: ["It guarantees that practitioners receive higher salaries than those in conventional medical fields.", "It automatically eliminates all commercial or unregulated versions of the practice.", "It shows that physical health, mental well-being, and energy flow are deeply interconnected.", "It can encourage governments to fund training programs and document traditional knowledge before it disappears."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of traditional Thai massage (Nuad Thai)?", options: ["Assisted stretching", "Application of aromatic oils", "Use of elbows, knees, and feet", "Rhythmic pressure along energy lines"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"inscribed\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["widely practiced", "formally announced", "officially listed", "permanently banned"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"diluted\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["weakened", "destroyed", "interconnected", "translated"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["yoga, acupuncture, and Thai massage", "regions of Asia", "distinct methods", "cultural contexts"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the relationship between UNESCO recognition and the future of these traditional practices?", options: ["Recognition guarantees that commercialized versions of these practices will completely disappear in the coming years.", "Recognition was granted only after each government agreed to ban Western-style adaptations of the practices.", "Recognition mainly benefits practitioners financially rather than helping preserve cultural knowledge for future generations.", "Recognition may help sustain these traditions, but growing global demand could still push them toward simplified, commercialized forms."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall stance toward the growing global popularity of these traditional healing practices?", options: ["Purely neutral, presenting no concerns about how recognition might affect these traditions", "Strongly dismissive, treating these practices as unscientific superstition", "Focused solely on praising the medical effectiveness of acupuncture", "Cautiously optimistic, acknowledging genuine benefits of recognition while noting risks of commercialization"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Air Pollution Indices and the Climate Change Debate", paragraphs: ["Over the past decade, growing alarm over climate change has become closely intertwined with public concern about air pollution, as governments increasingly rely on air quality indices to track two different but related problems at once: the immediate health risks posed by dirty air and the longer-term threat of a warming planet. This overlap is not accidental. Many of the same industrial processes and fuel-burning activities that release the greenhouse gases responsible for climate change also emit fine particulate matter and toxic gases that make air quality indices climb. As a result, a growing number of policymakers now treat a falling pollution index as a rough proxy for a shrinking climate footprint, even though the two measurements were originally designed for entirely separate purposes.", "The most widely used pollution index, generally known as the Air Quality Index, or AQI, was originally developed to warn the public about immediate health hazards rather than to track long-term climate trends. Calculated primarily from concentrations of fine particulate matter known as PM2.5, alongside ground-level ozone and a handful of other pollutants, the index converts complex scientific measurements into a single number that ordinary citizens can quickly interpret when deciding whether it is safe to exercise outdoors. Since its introduction several decades ago, the formula behind the index has been revised numerous times as scientists have gained a deeper understanding of which pollutants pose the greatest danger to human lungs and hearts.", "Despite these different origins, a considerable amount of overlap exists between the sources of particulate pollution and the sources of greenhouse gas emissions. Coal-fired power plants, for instance, release both carbon dioxide, which traps heat in the atmosphere, and sulfur compounds, which directly worsen the pollution index. Vehicle exhaust behaves similarly, contributing simultaneously to smog and to the broader accumulation of heat-trapping gases. Because of this shared origin, environmental economists frequently point to what they call a co-benefit: any policy that successfully lowers a city's pollution index, such as restricting older diesel vehicles, is also likely, though not guaranteed, to reduce that city's overall carbon footprint.", "Several cities offer instructive examples of this connection in practice. After tightening restrictions on coal burning and industrial emissions within its city limits, Beijing recorded a marked decline in its average pollution index over roughly a decade, a shift that coincided with a slower rate of growth in the city's carbon emissions compared with the previous decade. A broadly comparable pattern later emerged in Delhi, where a shift toward compressed natural gas for public buses lowered particulate readings even as citywide emissions data suggested a more modest, though still measurable, easing of overall greenhouse output. Such cases are frequently cited by policymakers as evidence that pollution-index targets can serve as an achievable, publicly popular entry point into more ambitious climate policy.", "Not every specialist, however, accepts this reasoning without reservation. Some environmental economists warn that falling pollution indices do not automatically guarantee comparable progress on climate change, since certain measures that clean up visible air pollution barely touch carbon output. Switching a power plant from coal to natural gas, for example, sharply cuts particulate emissions and can noticeably improve a city's pollution index, yet natural gas still releases substantial amounts of carbon dioxide when burned, meaning the climate benefit is considerably smaller than the visible improvement in air quality might suggest. Critics argue that treating the pollution index as an interchangeable stand-in for climate progress risks giving policymakers, and the public, a misleadingly optimistic picture of how much has actually been achieved toward long-term emissions targets.", "A further complication concerns how pollution data are actually gathered. Monitoring stations are disproportionately concentrated in wealthier urban districts, where local governments and residents have historically had greater political influence to demand measurement infrastructure, leaving poorer or more rural regions comparatively underrepresented in official statistics. Satellite-based measurements have partly filled this gap in recent years, offering broader geographic coverage, though they generally provide less precise, less granular readings than ground-based monitors and cannot easily distinguish between pollutants originating from fossil fuels and those from other sources, such as dust storms or agricultural burning happening far from any monitoring station.", "Looking ahead, a growing number of researchers argue that pollution indices and climate metrics should eventually be integrated into a single, unified reporting framework rather than being tracked and publicized as separate figures. Such integration, proponents suggest, would allow policymakers to design interventions that reliably deliver benefits on both fronts simultaneously, rather than assuming that success on one measurement automatically implies success on the other. Whether governments will adopt such a combined framework in the near future, or continue relying on pollution indices as a convenient but imperfect proxy for climate action, remains an open and actively debated question among scientists and policymakers alike."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage argues that pollution indices are usually unreliable and should never be used to track climate policy.", "The passage demonstrates that satellite monitoring has now completely replaced ground-based pollution measurement worldwide.", "The passage explains how the AQI formula has changed several times since its creation.", "Pollution indices and climate change metrics overlap in meaningful ways, offering real but imperfect value as tools for tracking climate progress."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the AQI primarily measure to generate its single number?", options: ["The total energy output of a city's coal-fired power plants", "Concentrations of PM2.5 alone, without factoring in ozone or other gases", "The total volume of carbon dioxide released by a city's power plants", "Concentrations of PM2.5, ground-level ozone, and a handful of other pollutants"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what change occurred in Beijing after it tightened restrictions on coal burning?", options: ["A marked decline in its pollution index, coinciding with a slower rate of growth in carbon emissions compared with the previous decade", "Particulate readings fell and citywide data suggested a modest but measurable easing of overall greenhouse output", "The change happened abruptly within a single year", "Carbon emissions fell in absolute terms across the entire city"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what change was associated with Delhi's shift to compressed natural gas buses?", options: ["Emissions from public transport were eliminated entirely", "Particulate readings fell and citywide data suggested a modest but measurable easing of overall greenhouse output", "The city recorded a marked decline in its pollution index after tightening restrictions on coal burning and industrial emissions", "Private car owners were required to switch to natural gas"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a source of overlap between air pollution and greenhouse gas emissions?", options: ["Agricultural burning, which the passage cites as another major source of overlap between air pollution and greenhouse gas emissions", "Cement manufacturing plants", "Coal-fired power plants", "Vehicle exhaust systems, which the passage identifies as the single largest combined contributor to both smog and greenhouse gases"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"proxy\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["guarantee", "monitor", "substitute", "consequence"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"granular\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["accurate", "regulated", "detailed", "expensive"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"Such cases\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["coal-fired power plants", "greenhouse output", "monitoring stations", "Beijing and Delhi"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the reliability of AQI improvements as an indicator of climate progress?", options: ["Because monitoring stations are concentrated in wealthy districts, wealthier cities generally show greater climate progress than poorer regions", "AQI improvements reliably indicate proportional reductions in greenhouse gas emissions in every case", "Satellite data proves that AQI has always underestimated the true severity of climate change in rural regions", "AQI improvements can indicate real climate progress, but the size of the climate benefit may be considerably smaller than the improvement in air quality suggests, particularly when fuel-switching rather than genuine emissions cuts drives the change"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in discussing both the co-benefits and the limitations of using pollution indices as climate proxies?", options: ["To describe in detail the technical formula used to calculate the AQI", "To strongly condemn the use of pollution indices in any climate-policy decision", "To criticize wealthy cities for dominating pollution-monitoring infrastructure", "To present a balanced view, showing that pollution indices can support climate policy while cautioning against treating them as a fully reliable substitute for direct emissions tracking"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Global Organic Food Market: Growth, Skepticism, and Regulatory Challenges", paragraphs: ["Global sales of organic food have expanded dramatically over the past two decades, transforming what was once a niche market catering to a small subset of environmentally conscious consumers into a multibillion-dollar industry embraced by mainstream retailers worldwide. This growth has been driven less by a single dominant motive than by a convergence of overlapping concerns: anxiety over pesticide exposure, a desire to support more sustainable farming methods, and, increasingly, a belief that organic products offer superior nutritional value. Not only have supermarket chains dramatically expanded their organic offerings in response to this demand, but many conventional food conglomerates have also begun acquiring smaller organic producers outright, a development that has itself become a source of considerable controversy within the movement.", "Complicating this growth, however, is the absence of any single, universally accepted definition of what qualifies as organic. Certification standards differ substantially from one jurisdiction to another; the criteria enforced in the United States diverge in several notable respects from those applied within the European Union, and many developing economies operate under still looser, less rigorously audited frameworks. This regulatory fragmentation arguably undermines consumer confidence, since a product labeled organic in one country may not satisfy the requirements that would earn it the same label elsewhere, leaving shoppers to rely on labeling systems whose rigor they have little practical means of verifying.", "Health claims associated with organic food remain similarly contested. Numerous studies have found that organically grown produce tends to carry measurably lower pesticide residue than its conventionally grown counterpart, a finding that is rarely disputed among researchers. Whether this translates into meaningfully superior nutritional value, however, is a separate and considerably more disputed question; several large-scale reviews have concluded that any nutritional differences, where they exist at all, are modest at best and may not justify the health claims frequently made in organic marketing. Consumers who purchase organic food primarily for its presumed nutritional superiority may, in other words, be paying a premium for a benefit that current evidence does not necessarily support.", "Environmental arguments in favor of organic agriculture are, in certain respects, on firmer empirical ground, though not without their own complications. Organic farming practices generally avoid synthetic pesticides and fertilizers, and numerous field studies have documented greater biodiversity, including higher populations of pollinating insects, on organic farmland compared with conventionally managed plots. Not only does this reduced reliance on synthetic inputs lower certain forms of chemical runoff into surrounding ecosystems, but it also tends to improve long-term soil health in ways that conventional monoculture farming frequently does not. Organic methods, however, typically produce lower yields per hectare than conventional farming, meaning that a full transition to organic agriculture would, according to several agronomists, require considerably more farmland to produce an equivalent volume of food, a trade-off that complicates any straightforward environmental endorsement.", "The price premium commanded by organic products, often ranging from twenty to fifty percent above conventional equivalents, has attracted its own share of criticism, with some commentators characterizing the organic movement as a form of elitism accessible mainly to affluent, health-conscious consumers. This premium, however, is not purely a function of marketing; organic certification requires costly annual audits, and organic farms typically incur higher labor costs due to their reliance on manual weeding and pest management rather than chemical alternatives. The premium therefore arguably reflects, at least in part, genuine differences in production cost rather than an entirely artificial markup, even if it simultaneously limits the accessibility of organic food for lower-income households.", "As global supply chains for organic goods have grown longer and more complex, opportunities for fraudulent labeling have expanded correspondingly. Investigations in several countries have uncovered cases in which conventionally grown grain was relabeled as organic somewhere along an international supply chain, particularly where oversight and enforcement capacity in exporting countries remain limited. Such incidents, while apparently not epidemic in scale, have nonetheless raised troubling questions about the practical reliability of organic certification once a product crosses multiple national borders before reaching a retail shelf, a vulnerability that undermines the very trust on which premium organic pricing depends.", "Perhaps the most philosophically fraught development within the industry concerns the growing involvement of large multinational food corporations, several of which have acquired formerly independent organic brands over the past fifteen years. Critics within the original organic movement argue that this consolidation risks diluting the ideological commitments, including small-scale farming and strong community ties, that initially distinguished organic agriculture from industrial food production. Defenders of consolidation counter that corporate investment provides organic producers with distribution networks and capital that would otherwise be unavailable to them , potentially making organic food more widely accessible than smaller independent producers could manage on their own; whether this expanded accessibility ultimately preserves or undermines the movement's founding values remains a matter of ongoing dispute rather than settled fact.", "Efforts toward international regulatory harmonization have progressed slowly, hindered by genuine disagreements over acceptable farming practices as well as by the divergent economic interests of exporting and importing nations. Some trade officials remain cautiously optimistic that mutual recognition agreements between major certification bodies will eventually reduce consumer confusion, though sceptics note that similar harmonization efforts in other agricultural sectors have taken decades to yield meaningful results. What emerges from this landscape is not a simple narrative of virtuous alternative triumphing over industrial food production, but rather an industry whose genuine environmental and ethical achievements sit uneasily alongside unresolved questions about verification, accessibility, and the compromises that scale increasingly demands."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Organic food will completely replace conventional food production within the next decade.", "The environmental benefits of organic farming outweigh all of the industry's other complications.", "The organic food industry has achieved genuine environmental and market gains, but continues to face unresolved challenges involving inconsistent standards, contested claims, fraud, and corporate consolidation.", "The passage explains how organic certification audits are conducted each year."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what price premium do organic products typically command over conventional equivalents?", options: ["Fifty to one hundred percent above conventional prices", "Roughly twenty to fifty percent above conventional prices", "A premium set collectively by international government regulators", "Measurably lower pesticide residue than conventional produce"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have numerous field studies documented on organic farmland?", options: ["Biodiversity levels roughly triple those found on conventional farmland", "Reduced rates of fraudulent labeling within the organic supply chain", "Greater biodiversity, including higher populations of pollinating insects, compared with conventionally managed plots", "Modest nutritional improvements over conventionally grown produce"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what development related to large food corporations has occurred over the past fifteen years?", options: ["Independent organic farms have begun acquiring shares in large multinational corporations", "Multinational corporations have relocated their organic production entirely to developing countries", "Conventional food conglomerates began acquiring organic producers only within the past two years", "Several major food conglomerates have acquired formerly independent organic brands"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a criticism or complication associated with the organic food industry?", options: ["Instances of fraudulent relabeling of conventional grain as organic during international transport", "The complete absence of any documented environmental benefits from organic farming", "Regulatory fragmentation, which the passage states has led several countries to abandon organic certification altogether", "Widespread consumer lawsuits filed against organic certification bodies"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"fragmentation\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["inconsistency", "premium", "breakdown", "expansion"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"consolidation\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["certification", "domination", "harvesting", "merging"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["small-scale farmers", "distribution networks and capital", "corporate investors", "organic producers"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what can be inferred about the relationship between organic food's price premium and its accessibility to different income groups?", options: ["Although the price premium partly reflects genuine production costs rather than pure profit-seeking, it nonetheless functions as a barrier that limits organic food's accessibility mainly to wealthier consumers, regardless of the industry's environmental or ethical achievements", "Organic farms typically rely more on manual labor for weeding and pest control than conventional farms", "Because organic farming yields are lower, all governments will eventually be forced to subsidize organic food for low-income consumers", "Lower-income consumers avoid organic food mainly because they doubt its environmental benefits, rather than because of its price"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in presenting both the achievements and unresolved problems of the organic food industry?", options: ["To dismiss the organic food movement entirely as an elitist marketing exercise", "To offer a balanced assessment acknowledging genuine environmental and ethical gains while highlighting unresolved tensions around verification, accessibility, and corporate consolidation", "To explain why certification standards differ between the United States and the European Union", "To condemn multinational corporations for acquiring independent organic brands"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
